From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dolbaras <dolbaras@localhost>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 15:15:51 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] hdmitx: PC monitors on HDMI

The bootloader showed no picture on a 1280x1024 monitor: it always set
the saved hdmimode (default 1080p60hz) without asking the sink, and
refused VESA modes altogether ("vout output 1280x1024p60hz" failed:
hdmi_outputmode_check() knew only TV modes).

- "hdmitx sink_mode" (run by the board after "hdmitx hpd", before
  "vout output"): reads and parses the EDID; if the sink does not list
  outputmode, or it is faster than the sink's range limits, this boot
  uses the sink's native mode (first detailed timing, else the listed
  VESA mode of its size and rate, else the preferred CEA mode ...).
  outputmode and hdmimode change in the running environment only.
- EDID: VESA modes from established timings, standard timings,
  Established Timings III and detailed timings; display range limits;
  native mode; the "first SVD" rule only with a CEA block; detailed
  timings whose clock has a zero low byte were skipped.
- output: the VESA modes and 2560x1080 (VIC 90) in the mode list; RGB
  8 bit for VESA modes and DVI sinks (the old check sat after a
  "break"); DVI mode for sinks without HDMI VSDB; sync polarity from the
  mode; PHY preset by TMDS rate; vout field_height for VESA modes,
  2560x1440p60hz added.
- OSD window of VESA modes: the position systemcontrol keeps for the
  mode (<mode>_x/_y/_w/_h, default the whole screen) instead of the
  1080p one, and the 1920x1080 logo / boot menu keeps its aspect ratio
  inside it (1280x1024: 1280x720 with borders) instead of being
  stretched.
- tables as in VESA DMT and the kernel (port/tools/check_vesa_tables.py):
  800x600, 1024x768, 1280x960, 1360x768 pixel clocks/totals, sync
  polarities, 1280x800 normal blanking (83.5 MHz), 2560x1080/2560x1440
  real timings, encoder tables and HDMI PLL settings (3960000, 4830000).
